# Sanctum — scrub denylist (EXAMPLE)
#
# Copy this to `.scrub-denylist` and replace the placeholders with your real
# terms. `.scrub-denylist` is git-ignored and MUST stay that way — a denylist
# committed to a public repo publishes exactly the strings you are hiding.
#
#     cp .scrub-denylist.example .scrub-denylist
#
# One POSIX extended regex per line. Matching is case-insensitive.
# Blank lines and lines starting with '#' are ignored.
# A line starting with '!' is a PATH PREFIX to exclude from scanning — use it
# where a term is legitimate (a scoring keyword, an accepted reference).
#
# Use \b word boundaries on short or ambiguous terms to avoid false positives
# (without \b, a three-letter term matches inside unrelated words).
#
# Keep THIS file generic. Placeholders only — the moment a real term appears
# here, the example becomes the leak.
#
# Scope this to what is actually sensitive for YOUR repo. Over-broad lists get
# bypassed with --no-verify within a week, which is worse than no guard.

# --- Person ---
# yourfirstname
# yourlastname
# you@example\.com
# your-github-handle

# --- Affiliation you must not disclose (service, parent body, unit) ---
# \bPARENT-ORGANIZATION\b
# \bSERVICE-BRANCH\b
# \bUNIT-NAME\b
# \bUNIT-TYPE\b
#
# Also deny the doctrine vocabulary unique to that affiliation — redacting the
# name while leaving its jargon in place tells the reader what was removed.
# \bFRAMEWORK-ACRONYM\b

# --- Hosts, hostnames, lab names ---
# \bprod-collector-01\b
# \binternal\.example\.local\b

# --- Private network space ---
# \b10\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\b
# \b192\.168\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\b
# \b172\.(1[6-9]|2[0-9]|3[01])\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\b

# --- Credentials (belt and braces; .gitignore is the primary guard) ---
# ghp_[A-Za-z0-9]{20,}
# -----BEGIN [A-Z ]*PRIVATE KEY-----

# --- Excluded paths (a term here is legitimate, not a leak) ---
# !path/to/scoring-config.md
# !path/to/accepted-reference.md
